\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Paired evaluation design. (a) Full and restricted greedy selection share the graph, the propagation parameters $\theta $, the nested prefix of $W$ selection worlds, the seed budget $k$ and the tie rule. They differ only in the eligible set: the restricted method searches the pool $C(\tau ,q)$ of the $q$ top-ranked vertices, with scores computed at tolerance $\tau $. (b) Both selected sets are evaluated on a shared family of $M=10{,}000$ worlds, disjoint from the selection worlds. The outputs are the spread difference $\Delta U$ (restricted minus full), the final-set Jaccard similarity $J$ and the selection-time ratio $\rho _T$.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:evaluation-mechanism}{{1}{3}{Paired evaluation design. (a) Full and restricted greedy selection share the graph, the propagation parameters $\theta $, the nested prefix of $W$ selection worlds, the seed budget $k$ and the tie rule. They differ only in the eligible set: the restricted method searches the pool $C(\tau ,q)$ of the $q$ top-ranked vertices, with scores computed at tolerance $\tau $. (b) Both selected sets are evaluated on a shared family of $M=10{,}000$ worlds, disjoint from the selection worlds. The outputs are the spread difference $\Delta U$ (restricted minus full), the final-set Jaccard similarity $J$ and the selection-time ratio $\rho _T$}{figure.1}{}}
